  "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)x.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x)x$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}x+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}x+2{u}^{\u2032}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{\u2033}x+2{u}^{\u2032})-2x({u}^{\u2032}x+u)+2ux & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{\u2033}x+2{u}^{\u2032}-2{u}^{\u2032})-2ux+2ux & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}{u}^{\u2033} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2033}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{\u2032}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{\u2032}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{\u2032},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}={c}_{1}\\,\u27f9\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=ux$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=x({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$",
